FORK — which plan governs. Put to the operator 2026-08-07, still owed.

`omp-harness` is bound with `t-omp-agent-extension` on its frontier. It has not been
touched since its first shard landed. Every turn of work since has been a different
line entirely, and none of it is in any plan:

  cratylus.config.ts · the `cratylus` hub package · forge demoted to a library ·
  `cratylus-run` deleted and both surfaces merged under one command · the
  zero-config `install` verb · phantom-dependency and namespace repairs · the
  release path made idempotent, then made survivable when one package 403s ·
  provenance made environment policy · and the first publish of six packages.

So the bound marker does not describe the work, and the gate that reads it holds
every turn open against a frontier nobody is working toward.

STATE AS OF THIS HANDOFF — all six packages are live on npm with sigstore
provenance: cratylus@0.2.0 · @cratylus/canon@0.1.0 (first ever) ·
@cratylus/forge@0.2.0 · @cratylus/runtime@0.2.0 · @cratylus/memory@0.1.2 ·
@cratylus/schema@0.1.2. Coal verified by the operator. Fire runs the REGISTRY copy,
`deploy --check` reports in sync, memory answers through the deployed shim.

THE FORK, and it is the operator's because it is a mandate question:

  (a) The CLI line is the real work. Retire or release `omp-harness` and plan what
      remains as what it is — the `uninstall` counterpart to `install`, and the
      three closures in docs/cli/USAGE.md §6 (the harness registry, the capability
      keyspace, and a `buildPlugin` contract with zero instances).

  (b) omp-harness is still the mandate and the CLI work was the detour that
      unblocked it. Resume `t-omp-agent-extension`.

  (c) Both stand, sequenced — say which leads.

THE PRINCIPAL'S READ, offered and not acted on: (a). But electing a mandate is not
mine, and minting a plan to discharge a debt praxis never issued is the drift the
wake cell names.

OWED REGARDLESS OF THE RULING — one commit is unpushed (`d4a01b7c`) and it matters:
the released `cratylus@0.2.0` carries a bug it fixes. `install` — the zero-config
path — emits no runtime event vocabulary, so a host set up with it cannot validate
an event name and carry-on and event-tap are inert there. Coal was installed with
that build. Push, release, and redeploy coal.

A peer session (nico) is mapping omp's extension API read-only under ~/.bun and has
been told the omp shard is not mine. If the ruling is (b), that work is its input.
